<p id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular</span></p>
<table id="S1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}\ :\ \mu_{g}\ \mapsto\ t_{n}\mu_{g},\quad\quad\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}\ :\ \lambda_{1}\ \mapsto\ t_{1}^{-1}\lambda_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}\ :\ \mu_{g}\ \mapsto\ t_{n}\mu_{g},\quad\quad\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}\ :\ \lambda_{1}\ \mapsto\ t_{1}^{-1}\lambda_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us prove the first formula of Part (ii). The other formulas can be
either proved similarly or are restatements of particular cases of
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a>. We have <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime}:t_{1}\mapsto t^{\prime}_{1}=c_{1}^{-1}t_{1}c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime}:t_{1}\mapsto t^{\prime}_{1}=c_{1}^{-1}t_{1}c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{1}=t_{2}\cdots t_{n}(u_{k}^{-1}u_{g})^{-1}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}=t_{2}\cdots t_{n}(u_{k}^{-1}u_{g})^{-1}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})^{-1}t_{1}(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})=u_{g}^{-1}t_{1}u_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})^{-1}t_{1}(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})=u_{g}^{-1}t_{1}u_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}t^{\prime}_{1}\ &amp;=\ (u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1})t_{1}(\cdots)^{-1}\ =\ (u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]^{-1}u_{k-1}^{-1})t_{1}(\cdots)^{-1}\\
&amp;=\ (u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1})^{{}^{-1}}\,t_{1}\,(u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="1.278em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.278em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="1.278em">=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}t^{\prime}_{1}\ &amp;=\ (u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1})t_{1}(\cdots)^{-1}\ =\ (u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]^{-1}u_{k-1}^{-1})t_{1}(\cdots)^{-1}\\
&amp;=\ (u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1})^{{}^{-1}}\,t_{1}\,(u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Summing up the discussion made so far in this section we obtain the
following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us consider the set of all
Hurwitz systems
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf.
Definition <a href="#Thmdfn1" title="Definition 1. ‣ 1.2. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Let <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be the free group generated by the
symbols <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j},\rho_{ik},\tau_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j},\rho_{ik},\tau_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n-1,\;1\leq i\leq n,\;1\leq k\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n-1,\;1\leq i\leq n,\;1\leq k\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us consider the right action of
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the set of Hurwitz systems defined by the formulas for
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> (a)–(c) or
alternatively let us consider the left action of <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the set of
Hurwitz systems defined by the formulas for
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> (d)–(f).
Then the connected components of <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf.
<a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="1.1. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) correspond bijectively to the orbits of either action of
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and the connected components of <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>
correspond bijectively to the orbits of either associated action of
<math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the set of equivalence classes of Hurwitz systems modulo inner
automorphisms of <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})\to(Y-b_{0})^{(n)}-\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})\to(Y-b_{0})^{(n)}-\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is a topological
covering map where
a Hausdorff topology on <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu</a>]</cite> pp.545,546. According to the definition of product of arcs
Eq.(<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) the monodromy action of the fundamental group
<math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}((Y-b_{0})^{(n)}-\Delta,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}((Y-b_{0})^{(n)}-\Delta,D)</annotation></semantics></math> on the fiber over <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
is a right action. The identification of this fiber with the set of Hurwitz
systems, Proposition <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 1.6 (Birman). ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and the calculation of the braid moves of
the first type <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> yield the statement about the orbits of the right
action of <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the associated left action <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gx=xg^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gx=xg^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The orbits are the
same and according to Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 1.4. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> the braid moves of the second
type <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are inverse to
those of the first type
<math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. This shows the statement about
the orbits of the left action of <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(b_{0})\subset\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(b_{0})\subset\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="1.1. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is a Zariski dense open
subset, the connected components of <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> correspond
bijectively to those of <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d}^{(n)}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows the last
claim of the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The main lemma</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section we considered Hurwitz systems
<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in connection with the problem of
determining the connected components of the Hurwitz spaces. In this
section we replace <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by an arbitrary (possibly infinite) group
<math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdfn3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary group. We call
<math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> a Hurwitz
system with values in <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The formulas of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> make sense for an arbitrary group
<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. That <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}=(\sigma_{j}^{\prime})^{-1},\&gt;\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime}=(\rho_{ik}^{\prime})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}=(\sigma_{j}^{\prime})^{-1},\&gt;\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime}=(\rho_{ik}^{\prime})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}=(\tau_{ik}^{\prime})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}=(\tau_{ik}^{\prime})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
evident from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdfn4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call two Hurwitz systems with values in <math id="Thmdfn4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> braid-equivalent if one
is obtained from the other by a finite sequence of braid moves
<math id="Thmdfn4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmdfn4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n-1,\&gt;1\leq i\leq n,\&gt;1\leq k\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n-1,\&gt;1\leq i\leq n,\&gt;1\leq k\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the braid equivalence by <math id="Thmdfn4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainlem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary group and let
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hurwitz
system with values in <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}t_{i+1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}t_{i+1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the
subgroup of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> generated by
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for
every <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H</annotation></semantics></math> the given Hurwitz system is braid-equivalent to
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i}^{h},t_{i+1}^{h},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i}^{h},t_{i+1}^{h},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us fix
<math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\ldots,t_{i-1},t_{i+2},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}\subseteq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}\subseteq H</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset consisting of elements <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the statement of the lemma holds for an arbitrary pair
<math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})=(\tau,\tau^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})=(\tau,\tau^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepa1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepa1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1</span></span><span id="Thmstepa1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepa1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepa1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepa1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We claim <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math></em>. Let <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=ht_{i}h^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=ht_{i}h^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=t^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=t^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, so by assumption
<math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,t^{h},(t^{-1})^{h},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,t^{h},(t^{-1})^{h},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained from
<math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,t,t^{-1},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,t,t^{-1},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> by a sequence of braid moves. Then one can
obtain <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,t,t^{-1},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,t,t^{-1},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,t_{i},t_{i}^{-1},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,t_{i},t_{i}^{-1},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>
by the inverse sequence of braid moves. Thus <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{-1}\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{-1}\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1},h_{2}\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1},h_{2}\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,t_{i},t_{i}^{-1},\ldots)\sim(\ldots,t_{i}^{h_{1}},(t_{i}^{-1})^{h_{1}},\ldots)\sim(\ldots,t_{i}^{h_{1}h_{2}},(t_{i}^{-1})^{h_{1}h_{2}},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,t_{i},t_{i}^{-1},\ldots)\sim(\ldots,t_{i}^{h_{1}},(t_{i}^{-1})^{h_{1}},\ldots)\sim(\ldots,t_{i}^{h_{1}h_{2}},(t_{i}^{-1})^{h_{1}h_{2}},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="Thmstepa1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}h_{2}\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}h_{2}\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepa2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepa2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2</span></span><span id="Thmstepa2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepa2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepa2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepa2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\neq i,i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\neq i,i+1</annotation></semantics></math> the element <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></em>.
Applying a sequence of braid moves <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we can
move the adjacent pair <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> wherever we want among the
first <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements of the Hurwitz system without changing the other
elements. So move <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> to the left side of <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
we have
<math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1},t_{\ell})\sim(t_{i},t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i+1}t_{\ell})\sim(t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i}t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i+1}t_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1},t_{\ell})\sim(t_{i},t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i+1}t_{\ell})\sim(t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i}t_{\ell},t_{\ell}^{-1}t_{i+1}t_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then move the pair <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i}^{h},t_{i+1}^{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i}^{h},t_{i+1}^{h})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepa2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> back
to the initial position.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepa3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepa3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 3</span></span><span id="Thmstepa3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepa3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepa3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepa3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\ldots,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\ldots,g</annotation></semantics></math> the element
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></em>. First suppose that <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}=t_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}=t_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us perform a braid move <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. One
obtains
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)\sim(t^{\prime}_{1},t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu^{\prime}_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)\sim(t^{\prime}_{1},t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu^{\prime}_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{\prime}=a_{1}^{-1}t_{1}a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{\prime}=a_{1}^{-1}t_{1}a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1}=\linebreak u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}(u_{k}^{-1}u_{g})(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=\linebreak u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}(u_{k}^{-1}u_{g})(t_{2}\cdots t_{n})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}^{\prime}=(b_{1}^{-1}t_{1}^{-1}b_{1})\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}^{\prime}=(b_{1}^{-1}t_{1}^{-1}b_{1})\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us move <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}=t_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}=t_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the
first place using <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. One obtains
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1}^{-1},t_{1}t^{\prime}_{1}t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu^{\prime}_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1}^{-1},t_{1}t^{\prime}_{1}t_{1}^{-1},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu^{\prime}_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}t_{1}^{\prime}t_{1}^{-1}=t_{1}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}t_{1}^{\prime}t_{1}^{-1}=t_{1}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
us perform again <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The elements <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k-1},\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1},\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> have
not changed with respect to the original Hurwitz system, so
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}^{\prime}\mapsto[(u_{k-1}\lambda_{k})^{-1}(t_{1}^{-1})^{-1}(u_{k-1}\lambda_{k})]\mu_{k}^{\prime}=\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}^{\prime}\mapsto[(u_{k-1}\lambda_{k})^{-1}(t_{1}^{-1})^{-1}(u_{k-1}\lambda_{k})]\mu_{k}^{\prime}=\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moving the second element <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to the first place
by <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1}^{h},\tilde{t}_{2},t_{3},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1}^{h},\tilde{t}_{2},t_{3},\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m25" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}t_{2}t_{3}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}=t_{1}^{h}\tilde{t}_{2}t_{3}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}t_{2}t_{3}\cdots t_{n}=u_{g}=t_{1}^{h}\tilde{t}_{2}t_{3}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we must have <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}_{2}=(t_{1}^{-1})^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}_{2}=(t_{1}^{-1})^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves
<math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},t_{1}^{-1},t_{3},\ldots)\sim(t_{1}^{h},(t_{1}^{-1})^{h},t_{3},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},t_{1}^{-1},t_{3},\ldots)\sim(t_{1}^{h},(t_{1}^{-1})^{h},t_{3},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. One extends this braid
equivalence to every adjacent pair <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepa3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}t_{i+1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}t_{i+1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>
by moving first the pair to the front, applying the braid equivalence
we have just proved and moving the obtained pair back to the original
position.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepa4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepa4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 4</span></span><span id="Thmstepa4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepa4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepa4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepa4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmstepa4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\ldots,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\ldots,g</annotation></semantics></math> the element
<math id="Thmstepa4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="Thmstepa4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></em>. The proof is the
same as that of Step <a href="#Thmstepa3" title="Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. One uses the braid move
<math id="Thmstepa4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmstepa4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepa5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepa5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 5</span></span><span id="Thmstepa5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepa5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepa5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By the preceding steps it remains to verify that
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall k</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the subgroup
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}\subseteq H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}\subseteq H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j},u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1},u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j},u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1},u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,i-1,i+2,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,i-1,i+2,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\ldots,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\ldots,g</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove this by induction on <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}u_{1}^{-1}\in H_{2},\;u_{1}\mu_{1}=(\mu_{1}^{-1}u_{1}^{-1})^{-1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}u_{1}^{-1}\in H_{2},\;u_{1}\mu_{1}=(\mu_{1}^{-1}u_{1}^{-1})^{-1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\mu_{1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\mu_{1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}\ni u_{1}\mu_{1}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\mu_{1}=\lambda_{1}\mu_{1}\lambda_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∋</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}\ni u_{1}\mu_{1}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\mu_{1}=\lambda_{1}\mu_{1}\lambda_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{1}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{1}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose, by inductive
assumption, that <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{k-1},\mu_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{k-1},\mu_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k-1}=\prod_{\ell=1}^{k-1}[\lambda_{\ell},\mu_{\ell}]\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1}=\prod_{\ell=1}^{k-1}[\lambda_{\ell},\mu_{\ell}]\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}u_{k}^{-1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}=(u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1})^{-1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}=(u_{k-1}\mu_{k}^{-1}u_{k}^{-1})^{-1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1}\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}\ni u_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}=u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∋</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}\ni u_{k}\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}=u_{k-1}[\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}]\mu_{k}u_{k-1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}\lambda_{k}^{-1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\mu_{k}\lambda_{k}^{-1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{k}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepa5.p1.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{k}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}^{{}^{\pm 1}}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lemma is proved.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The case <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have not made any restrictions on the type of the covering
<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall be further occupied mainly with simply branched
coverings with connected <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. These coverings correspond to
Hurwitz systems
<math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with local
monodromies <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=m(\gamma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=m(\gamma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> equal to
transpositions and with transitive monodromy group <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. We call such coverings <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simple coverings</em>. By Hurwitz’
formula <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 0(mod\;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 0(mod\;2)</annotation></semantics></math> for a simple covering.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdfn5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth projective curve. We denote by
<math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> the Hurwitz
spaces which parametrize equivalence classes <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[X\to Y,\&gt;\phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X\to Y,\&gt;\phi]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[X\to Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X\to Y]</annotation></semantics></math> respectively of simple coverings of degree <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> branched in <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points
(cf. <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="1.1. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a first application of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> we give a proof, using
braid moves, of the following well-known fact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_pro">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\&gt;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\&gt;2)</annotation></semantics></math>. The Hurwitz spaces
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which
parametrize double coverings of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> branched in <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points are both
irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth
it suffices to prove they are connected. Let us first consider
<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> it suffices to
prove that every Hurwitz system
<math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to
<math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),\ldots,(12);1,1,\ldots,\linebreak 1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),\ldots,(12);1,1,\ldots,\linebreak 1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a>
one has <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ik}^{\prime}:(t_{i},\mu_{k})\mapsto(t_{i},t_{i}\mu_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ik}^{\prime}:(t_{i},\mu_{k})\mapsto(t_{i},t_{i}\mu_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ik}^{\prime}:(t_{i},\lambda_{k})\mapsto(t_{i},t_{i}\lambda_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ik}^{\prime}:(t_{i},\lambda_{k})\mapsto(t_{i},t_{i}\lambda_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and the
same formulas hold for <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence whenever
some <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we can perform a braid move
<math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively or both in order to obtain a new Hurwitz
system with <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}^{\prime}=\mu_{k}^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}^{\prime}=\mu_{k}^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The connectedness of
<math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> implies the connectedness of the Zariski dense
open subset <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(b_{0})\subset\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(b_{0})\subset\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
<math id="S3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{2,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is connected as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given an ordered <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of permutations <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
whose product <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, performing an elementary
move <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> one obtains a new ordered
<math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,t_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,t_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> which has the same product <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}^{\prime}\cdots t_{n}^{\prime}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{\prime}\cdots t_{n}^{\prime}=s</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall also call ordered <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdfn6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two ordered <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples (or sequences) of permutations
<math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}=(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,t_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}=(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,t_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are called braid-equivalent if
<math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by a finite sequence
of braid moves of type <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write
<math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}\sim\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}\sim\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notice the difference between braid equivalence of sequences and that
of Hurwitz systems (cf. Definition <a href="#Thmdfn4" title="Definition 4. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The latter includes
also braid moves of the types
<math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_block">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall some results proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite>. Given a permutation <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> one considers an ordered <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of transpositions
<math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of transitivity
of the group <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G=S(\Sigma_{1})\times\cdots\times S(\Sigma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=S(\Sigma_{1})\times\cdots\times S(\Sigma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. For reader’s convenience we
give a proof of the following important lemma due to Mochizuki (cf.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite> Lemma 2.4).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}),\;t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}),\;t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s</annotation></semantics></math> be as
above. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\Sigma_{i},\;a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\Sigma_{i},\;a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}\sim\underline{t}^{\prime}=((ab),t^{\prime}_{2},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∼</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}\sim\underline{t}^{\prime}=((ab),t^{\prime}_{2},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}\sim\underline{t}^{\prime\prime}=(\ldots,t^{\prime\prime}_{n-1},(ab))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∼</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}\sim\underline{t}^{\prime\prime}=(\ldots,t^{\prime\prime}_{n-1},(ab))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair such that <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in S(\Sigma_{k}),\;t_{i+1}\in S(\Sigma_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in S(\Sigma_{k}),\;t_{i+1}\in S(\Sigma_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},t_{i+1})\sim(t_{i+1},t_{i+1}^{-1}t_{i}t_{i+1})=(t_{i+1},t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},t_{i+1})\sim(t_{i+1},t_{i+1}^{-1}t_{i}t_{i+1})=(t_{i+1},t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that
performing a
sequence of braid moves (of type <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) one may
replace <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by a concatenation of sequences <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}T_{2}\ldots T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}T_{2}\ldots T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is formed by all transpositions of <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
which belong to <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> ordered in the way they appear in
<math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>).
Furthermore we may move any <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the first place or to the last place.
This shows that it
suffices to prove the lemma in the particular case when <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a transitive group. Let <math id="S3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=a^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=a^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.p6.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau=t_{j_{1}}t_{j_{2}}\cdots t_{j_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=t_{j_{1}}t_{j_{2}}\cdots t_{j_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One may vary the
sequence within the set of sequences braid-equivalent to
<math id="S3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a sequence one may vary <math id="S3.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> so that
<math id="S3.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=a^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=a^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and finally given a sequence and a <math id="S3.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> one may vary
the factorization of <math id="S3.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property
<math id="S3.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\tau^{\prime}}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\tau^{\prime}}=b</annotation></semantics></math> and a factorization
<math id="S3.p6.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau^{\prime}=t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\cdots t^{\prime}_{i_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}=t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\cdots t^{\prime}_{i_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> be chosen so that <math id="S3.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal
possible. If <math id="S3.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> one has that <math id="S3.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the transpositions of
<math id="S3.p6.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moving <math id="S3.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> to the front by subsequent
elementary moves <math id="S3.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> one obtains a braid-equivalent
sequence of the type <math id="S3.p6.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moving <math id="S3.p6.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> to the end by
subsequent
elementary moves <math id="S3.p6.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> one obtains <math id="S3.p6.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,(ab))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,(ab))</annotation></semantics></math> as required
in the lemma. Suppose that <math id="S3.p6.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p6.m38" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1}=a,\,x_{2}=a^{t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}},\ldots,x_{k+1}=a^{t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\cdots t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=a,\,x_{2}=a^{t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}},\ldots,x_{k+1}=a^{t^{\prime}_{i_{1}}\cdots t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b</annotation></semantics></math>. The
minimality of <math id="S3.p6.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S3.p6.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\neq x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\neq x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p6.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.p6.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}=(x_{k}\,x_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}=(x_{k}\,x_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p6.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\ldots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\ldots,r</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.p6.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> different
transpositions of the sequence <math id="S3.p6.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S3.p6.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.p6.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> enters in at most two transpositions of the set
<math id="S3.p6.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t^{\prime}_{i_{k}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying several
elementary moves one
places <math id="S3.p6.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ax_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ax_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to <math id="S3.p6.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2}x_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2}x_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. One obtains a sequence either
of the type <math id="S3.p6.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots,(a\,x_{2}),(x_{2}x_{3}),\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots,(a\,x_{2}),(x_{2}x_{3}),\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> or of the type
<math id="S3.p6.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots,(x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{2}),\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots,(x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{2}),\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> in which none of <math id="S3.p6.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{i_{k}},\,k=1,\ldots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{i_{k}},\,k=1,\ldots,r</annotation></semantics></math> has been changed. In the first case one has
<math id="S3.p6.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((a\,x_{2}),(x_{2}x_{3}))\sim((x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{3}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((a\,x_{2}),(x_{2}x_{3}))\sim((x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{3}))</annotation></semantics></math> and in the second case
<math id="S3.p6.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{2}))\sim((a\,x_{3}),(x_{2}x_{3}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((x_{2}x_{3}),(a\,x_{2}))\sim((a\,x_{3}),(x_{2}x_{3}))</annotation></semantics></math>. In both cases one
obtains a braid-equivalent sequence for which the sequence
<math id="S3.p6.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by
<math id="S3.p6.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=x_{1},x_{3},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=x_{1},x_{3},\ldots,x_{r+1}=b</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts the minimality of <math id="S3.p6.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_block">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of transpositions and let
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
domains of transitivity of the cyclic group <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Delta_{i}=e_{i}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Delta_{i}=e_{i}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite> pp.369,370 if
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=s_{1}\cdots s_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=s_{1}\cdots s_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding product of independent cycles
we may write <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=(1_{i}2_{i}\ldots(e_{i})_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=(1_{i}2_{i}\ldots(e_{i})_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=(1_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=(1_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a representation is uniquely determined by <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> if we
assume that that <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimal number among <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and if we order <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{1}&lt;1_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;1_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{1}&lt;1_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;1_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sequence
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1_{i}2_{i}),(1_{i}3_{i}),\ldots,(1_{i}(e_{i})_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1_{i}2_{i}),(1_{i}3_{i}),\ldots,(1_{i}(e_{i})_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> one lets
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> be the concatenation of
sequences <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}=\mathcal{Z}_{1}\mathcal{Z}_{2}\ldots\mathcal{Z}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}=\mathcal{Z}_{1}\mathcal{Z}_{2}\ldots\mathcal{Z}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sequence <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of
<math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\sum_{i=1}^{q}(e_{i}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\sum_{i=1}^{q}(e_{i}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> transpositions whose product equals <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The
following theorem is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite> pp.369,370. It can also be
deduced from the earlier paper of Kluitmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a sequence of transpositions <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a transitive group (therefore = <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>), there is a
braid-equivalent sequence <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> which has the following
form</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})\ =\ (\mathcal{Z},t^{\prime}_{N+1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})\ =\ (\mathcal{Z},t^{\prime}_{N+1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-N\equiv 0(mod\,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-N\equiv 0(mod\,2)</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{i}=(1_{1}2_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{i}=(1_{1}2_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i\geq N+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\geq N+1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{{}_{N+1}},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})\ =\ ((1_{1}1_{2}),(1_{1}1_{2}),(1_{1}1_{3}),(1_{1}1_{3}),\ldots,(1_{1}1_{q}),(1_{1}1_{q}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{{}_{N+1}},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})\ =\ ((1_{1}1_{2}),(1_{1}1_{2}),(1_{1}1_{3}),(1_{1}1_{3}),\ldots,(1_{1}1_{q}),(1_{1}1_{q}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where each </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1}1_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1}1_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> appears two times if </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq q-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1}1_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1}1_{q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> appears an even number of times .</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of transpositions
in <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which generate a transitive group. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and let <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of transitivity
of the cyclic group <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Delta_{i},\;b\in\Delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Delta_{i},\;b\in\Delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
is braid-equivalent to a sequence <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,(ab),(ab))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,(ab),(ab))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us consider the group <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> which permutes the indices of <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i},\,i=1,\ldots,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i},\,i=1,\ldots,q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}=(ij),\tau_{2},\ldots,\tau_{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}=(ij),\tau_{2},\ldots,\tau_{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be transpositions in
<math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> which generate it. We let <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=a,\;a_{j}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=a,\;a_{j}=b</annotation></semantics></math> and choose
arbitrarily <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}\in\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}\in\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq i,j</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\ell}=(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\ell}=(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> we consider the pair of transpositions
<math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\alpha}a_{\beta}),(a_{\alpha}a_{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\alpha}a_{\beta}),(a_{\alpha}a_{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then consider a
sequence of <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> transpositions in <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a concatenation of the
sequence
<math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, of the pairs associated with
<math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1},\tau_{2},\ldots,\tau_{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1},\tau_{2},\ldots,\tau_{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and of the sequence <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\,2),\ldots,(1\,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1 2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1 2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\,2),\ldots,(1\,2)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(12)</annotation></semantics></math> repeated
an even number of times. We obtain a sequence
<math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z},(ab),(ab),\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z},(ab),(ab),\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> with product <math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> which generates a
transitive group. According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> this sequence is
braid-equivalent to <math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moving the pair <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab),(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab),(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> to the end we
obtain
the required sequence.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_block">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a permutation <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> whose domains of transitivity are
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Delta_{i}=e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Delta_{i}=e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> we let
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|=\sum_{i=1}^{q}(e_{i}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|=\sum_{i=1}^{q}(e_{i}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us consider a sequence of
transpositions <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}),\;t_{i}\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}),\;t_{i}\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of
transitivity of the group <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. We
let <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\Sigma}|=\sum_{j=1}^{m}(\#\,\Sigma_{j}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\Sigma}|=\sum_{j=1}^{m}(\#\,\Sigma_{j}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of transpositions
and let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=s</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\equiv 0(mod\,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\equiv 0(mod\,2)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2|\underline{\Sigma}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2|\underline{\Sigma}|</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|&gt;2|\underline{\Sigma}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|&gt;2|\underline{\Sigma}|</annotation></semantics></math> then there is a sequence
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> braid-equivalent to <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math> generate the same group
as <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that every <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to some <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore one may replace <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent
sequence which is a concatenation <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}T_{2}\ldots T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}T_{2}\ldots T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of sequences
<math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> whose elements belong to <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. the proof of
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>; if <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> one lets <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>). It
thus suffices to prove the lemma in the case when <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive, which we further assume, so
<math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\},\;|\underline{\Sigma}|=d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\},\;|\underline{\Sigma}|=d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us
consider the braid-equivalent sequence <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. The subsequence <math id="S3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=|s|</annotation></semantics></math>
elements. The remaining <math id="S3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-|s|</annotation></semantics></math> elements appear in pairs and their
number is at least <math id="S3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a transitive group. If <math id="S3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-|s|&gt;2(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-|s|&gt;2(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> the pair
<math id="S3.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1}1_{q}),(1_{1}1_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1}1_{q}),(1_{1}1_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is repeated at least twice, so canceling it from
the sequence does not change the group generated by the transpositions <math id="S3.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{t^{\prime}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{t^{\prime}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One has <math id="S3.p8.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\cup_{i=1}^{q}\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\cup_{i=1}^{q}\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=|s|+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=|s|+q</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding
<math id="S3.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|s|</annotation></semantics></math> to both sides of the inequality <math id="S3.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-|s|\geq 2(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-|s|\geq 2(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain
the equivalent inequality <math id="S3.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2=2|\Sigma_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2=2|\Sigma_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmdfn7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A transitive subgroup <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="Thmdfn7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">imprimitive</em> if
there is a decomposition <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall g\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math> one has
<math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}=\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}=\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and furthermore <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{i}=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{i}=e</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;e&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;e&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. A transitive subgroup
<math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which is not imprimitive is called <em id="Thmdfn7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primitive</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If
<math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime number clearly every transitive subgroup of <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a primitive group which contains a
transposition. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular if <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime number
every transitive subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which contains a transposition is
equal to <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us prove that if <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a nontrivial symmetric subgroup
<math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma^{\prime}=\#\,\Sigma+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma^{\prime}=\#\,\Sigma+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, since <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive
there are <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,h\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,h\in G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\#(\Sigma^{g}\cap\Sigma^{h})&lt;\#\,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\#(\Sigma^{g}\cap\Sigma^{h})&lt;\#\,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence if <math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=hg^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=hg^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\#(\Sigma\cap\Sigma^{f})&lt;\#\,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\#(\Sigma\cap\Sigma^{f})&lt;\#\,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{f}\in\Sigma\cap\Sigma^{f},\;y^{f}\in\Sigma^{f}-\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{f}\in\Sigma\cap\Sigma^{f},\;y^{f}\in\Sigma^{f}-\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\Sigma,\;x^{f}\in\Sigma,\;y^{f}\notin\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\Sigma,\;x^{f}\in\Sigma,\;y^{f}\notin\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\Sigma\cup\{x,y\}\supsetneqq\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⫌</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\Sigma\cup\{x,y\}\supsetneqq\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma^{\prime})=\langle S(\Sigma),f^{-1}(xy)f\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma^{\prime})=\langle S(\Sigma),f^{-1}(xy)f\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in
<math id="S3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude the proof of the lemma by induction on <math id="S3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
starting from <math id="S3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\in G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">To each connected component of <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is
associated a fixed monodromy group <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly to
each connected component of <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> is associated a
conjugacy class of subgroups.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmdfn8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y))</annotation></semantics></math> the union of connected components of
<math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{(n)}_{d}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{(n)}_{d}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{(n)}_{d}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{(n)}_{d}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively
which correspond to coverings with primitive
monodromy groups (therefore equal to <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 3.9. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem is due in the case <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2d</annotation></semantics></math> to Graber, Harris
and Starr <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">GHS</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, projective curve. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>
the Hurwitz spaces <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The case <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is classical and due to Clebsch and Hurwitz
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cl</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu</a>]</cite>. Modern proofs may be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite> p.368
and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vo</a>]</cite> p.197. Suppose that <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us first consider
<math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth in order to prove its irreducibility it suffices to prove it is
connected. By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> it suffices to show that every
Hurwitz system
<math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with
monodromy group <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1d),(1d);1,1,\ldots,1,1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1d),(1d);1,1,\ldots,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where each <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1i)</annotation></semantics></math> appears twice if <math id="S3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>
appears <math id="S3.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2d+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2d+4</annotation></semantics></math> times.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepb1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepb">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepb1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1</span></span><span id="Thmstepb1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepb1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepb1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that every Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is
braid-equivalent to a Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a
transitive group. Indeed, let <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of
transitivity of the group <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. One
has <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\Sigma}|=d-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\Sigma}|=d-m</annotation></semantics></math>, so if <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is not transitive then <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\Sigma}|&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\Sigma}|&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this
case the inequality <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|&gt;2|\underline{\Sigma}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|&gt;2|\underline{\Sigma}|</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied, so
according to Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> one may replace <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a
braid-equivalent sequence <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}=(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}=(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2}\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2}\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{h}\in\Sigma_{1},\;b^{h}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h}\in\Sigma_{1},\;b^{h}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then according to Main
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Main Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> the Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},t^{\prime}_{n-1},t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},t^{\prime}_{n-1},t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},(t^{\prime}_{n-1})^{h},(t^{\prime}_{n})^{h};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},(t^{\prime}_{n-1})^{h},(t^{\prime}_{n})^{h};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}^{\prime}=\{\Sigma^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\Sigma^{\prime}_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}^{\prime}=\{\Sigma^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\Sigma^{\prime}_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are the domains
of transitivity of <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},(t^{\prime}_{n-1})^{h},(t^{\prime}_{n})^{h}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n-2},(t^{\prime}_{n-1})^{h},(t^{\prime}_{n})^{h}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> then clearly <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\Sigma}^{\prime}|&gt;|\underline{\Sigma}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\Sigma}^{\prime}|&gt;|\underline{\Sigma}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Repeating this argument after a finite number of steps one obtains a
braid-equivalent Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with transitive <math id="Thmstepb1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepb2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepb">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepb2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2</span></span><span id="Thmstepb2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepb2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepb2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> be a
Hurwitz system. Suppose that
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda_{\ell}|+|\mu_{\ell}|)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda_{\ell}|+|\mu_{\ell}|)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim there
is a braid-equivalent sequence
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda^{\prime}_{1},\mu^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\lambda^{\prime}_{g},\mu^{\prime}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda^{\prime}_{1},\mu^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\lambda^{\prime}_{g},\mu^{\prime}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> such
that
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda^{\prime}_{\ell}|+|\mu^{\prime}_{\ell}|)&lt;\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda_{\ell}|+|\mu_{\ell}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda^{\prime}_{\ell}|+|\mu^{\prime}_{\ell}|)&lt;\sum_{\ell=1}^{g}(|\lambda_{\ell}|+|\mu_{\ell}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us first suppose that <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Decomposing <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
into a product of nontrivial independent cycles and choosing one of
them <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab\ldots c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab\ldots c)</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(ab)\lambda_{1}|&lt;|\lambda_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(ab)\lambda_{1}|&lt;|\lambda_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to Step <a href="#Thmstepb1" title="Step 1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we may replace
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> by a
braid-equivalent system
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> generate a transitive
group. Then according to Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we may replace
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent sequence
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),t^{\prime}_{2},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),t^{\prime}_{2},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the braid move <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf.
Eq.(<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)) we transform <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=1,\;\mu_{1}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=1,\;\mu_{1}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We reason in the same way
and use the braid move <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{11}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{11}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which transforms <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),\ldots;1,\mu_{1},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),\ldots;1,\mu_{1},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots;1,(ab)\mu_{1},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots;1,(ab)\mu_{1},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>
according to Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm9" title="Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a> (i). Similarly if <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\ell\leq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\ell\leq k-1</annotation></semantics></math> we
have <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and applying the braid move <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we transform
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),\ldots;\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),\ldots;\ldots,\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots;\ldots,(ab)\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots;\ldots,(ab)\lambda_{k},\mu_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> thus decreasing
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{k}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{k}|</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\ell\leq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\ell\leq k-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math> we apply <math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to transform
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),\ldots;\ldots,1,\mu_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),\ldots;\ldots,1,\mu_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots;\ldots,1,(ab)\mu_{k},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1}^{\prime},\ldots;\ldots,1,(ab)\mu_{k},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> thus decreasing
<math id="Thmstepb2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{k}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{k}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepb3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepb">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepb3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 3</span></span><span id="Thmstepb3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepb3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepb3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Starting with
<math id="Thmstepb3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and applying
several times Step <a href="#Thmstepb2" title="Step 2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> one obtains a braid-equivalent Hurwitz
system <math id="Thmstepb3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};1,1,\ldots,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};1,1,\ldots,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here
<math id="Thmstepb3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{t}_{1}\cdots\tilde{t}_{n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}_{1}\cdots\tilde{t}_{n}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so applying the argument in the
case <math id="Thmstepb3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)=0</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite> p.368, or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vo</a>]</cite> p.197) one
obtains the initial Hurwitz system is braid-equivalent to
Eq.(<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The connectedness of both <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> follows now from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> may be generalized in a straightforward manner to
coverings <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> which have simple ramifications in all except
possibly one discriminant point. The conjugacy classes of <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
correspond bijectively to partitions of <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, namely
<math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{e}=\{e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{q}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{e}=\{e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{q}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq\ldots\geq e_{q}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq\ldots\geq e_{q}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+\cdots+e_{q}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+\cdots+e_{q}=d</annotation></semantics></math>. To each partition one
associates the orbit of the permutation</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=(1\,2\ldots e_{1})(e_{1}+1\ldots e_{1}+e_{2})\ldots(\sum_{i=1}^{q-1}e_{i}\&gt;+1\ldots d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=(1\,2\ldots e_{1})(e_{1}+1\ldots e_{1}+e_{2})\ldots(\sum_{i=1}^{q-1}e_{i}\&gt;+1\ldots d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.2" class="ltx_p">Given a partition <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{e}</annotation></semantics></math> let us denote by
<math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>) the Hurwitz spaces which
parametrize equivalence classes <math id="S3.p15.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[X\to Y,\&gt;\phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X\to Y,\&gt;\phi]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p15.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[X\to Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X\to Y]</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, where <math id="S3.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and <math id="S3.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a covering of degree
<math id="S3.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> discriminant points of simple ramification and one
additional discriminant point whose local monodromy belongs to the
conjugacy class of Eq.(<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Let
<math id="S3.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>
parametrize such
coverings with primitive monodromy group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, projective curve. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{e}</annotation></semantics></math> be an
arbitrary partition of <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Hurwitz spaces
<math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y,b_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n,\underline{e}}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the trivial partition (that is <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>)
this is the
content of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.
Suppose that <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us denote
the permutation Eq.(<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) by
<math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=(1_{1}2_{1}\ldots(e_{1})_{1})\linebreak(1_{2}\ldots(e_{2})_{2})\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=(1_{1}2_{1}\ldots(e_{1})_{1})\linebreak(1_{2}\ldots(e_{2})_{2})\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>. The
theorem will be proved if we can show that every Hurwitz system
<math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},t_{n+1};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},t_{n+1};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> of
the considered type may be reduced after a finite number of braid
moves of the types
<math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime},\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime},\rho_{ik}^{\prime\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime},\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to the normal form</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{Z},t_{N+1}^{\prime},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n},\epsilon^{-1};1,1,\ldots,1,1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{Z},t_{N+1}^{\prime},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n},\epsilon^{-1};1,1,\ldots,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of transpositions <math id="S3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{Z},t_{N+1}^{\prime},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{Z},t_{N+1}^{\prime},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the one defined in <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="3.4. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. First using braid moves of type <math id="S3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
we may replace the original Hurwitz system by one for which <math id="S3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
belongs to the orbit of <math id="S3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. We then let
<math id="S3.p16.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]t^{-1}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]t^{-1}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
repeating the arguments of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> we obtain
a braid-equivalent Hurwitz system
<math id="S3.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n},\tilde{t}_{n+1};1,1,\ldots,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n},\tilde{t}_{n+1};1,1,\ldots,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}_{n+1}=t_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}_{n+1}=t_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We are further allowed to apply only braid
moves of types <math id="S3.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The next step is to
replace the obtained Hurwitz system by one in which at the <math id="S3.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th
place stays <math id="S3.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact the permutation <math id="S3.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
has the same cyclic type as <math id="S3.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}=a^{-1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}=a^{-1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}a</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p16.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\tau_{1}\cdots\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S3.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are transpositions. By the hypothesis and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 3.9. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>
we have <math id="S3.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n}\rangle=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. So using
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we may replace
<math id="S3.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent <math id="S3.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple
<math id="S3.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots,\tau_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots,\tau_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have <math id="S3.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{1},\tilde{t}_{n+1})\sim(\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1},\tau_{1})\sim(\tau_{1}^{\prime},\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{1},\tilde{t}_{n+1})\sim(\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1},\tau_{1})\sim(\tau_{1}^{\prime},\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n},\tilde{t}_{n+1})\sim(\ldots,\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n},\tilde{t}_{n+1})\sim(\ldots,\tau_{1}\tilde{t}_{n+1}\tau_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Repeating this argument
with <math id="S3.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2},\tau_{3},\ldots,\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2},\tau_{3},\ldots,\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a braid-equivalent
<math id="S3.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple whose <math id="S3.p16.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th element is <math id="S3.p16.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
product of the first <math id="S3.p16.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S3.p16.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Using a finite number of
braid moves among the first <math id="S3.p16.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> transpositions we obtain the required
normal form according to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">So far we worked with coverings with primitive monodromy groups. We now
wish to consider the imprimitive case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a transitive imprimitive subgroup which
contains a transposition. Then there is a unique decomposition
<math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊔</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Definition <a href="#Thmdfn7" title="Definition 7. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> so that <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=G\cap S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=G\cap S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a primitive
subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore the following
properties hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">i.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All transpositions contained in </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> form one conjugacy </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-orbit
</span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">ii.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G_{1}\cdot G_{2}\cdots G_{k}\ =\ G_{1}\times G_{2}\times\cdots\times G_{k}=\langle T\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}\cdot G_{2}\cdots G_{k}\ =\ G_{1}\times G_{2}\times\cdots\times G_{k}=\langle T\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">iii.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(ab)^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(ab)^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\langle T\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\langle T\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of transitivity of <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup every <math id="S3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math> permutes the orbits
<math id="S3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore <math id="S3.p18.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H=S(\Sigma_{1})\times\cdots\times S(\Sigma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=S(\Sigma_{1})\times\cdots\times S(\Sigma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=G\cap S(\Sigma_{i})=S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=G\cap S(\Sigma_{i})=S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
All transpositions which belong to <math id="S3.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> form one <math id="S3.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit since this
property holds for <math id="S3.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha\,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha\,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> be an
arbitrary transposition of <math id="S3.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim it is impossible that
<math id="S3.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Sigma_{i},\;\beta\in\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Sigma_{i},\;\beta\in\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if
this were the case letting <math id="S3.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=(\alpha\,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=(\alpha\,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> we would have
<math id="S3.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}=\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{i}}^{g}=\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\in\Sigma_{i},\alpha^{\prime}\neq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\in\Sigma_{i},\alpha^{\prime}\neq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha^{\prime}}^{g}=\alpha^{\prime}\in\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>α</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha^{\prime}}^{g}=\alpha^{\prime}\in\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and on the other hand
<math id="S3.p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha^{\prime}}^{g}\in\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>α</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha^{\prime}}^{g}\in\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}\cap\Sigma_{j}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>
which is absurd. We obtain <math id="S3.p18.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha\,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha\,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
some <math id="S3.p18.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p18.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha\,\beta)\in T=(ab)^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha\,\beta)\in T=(ab)^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves (i). Suppose now that <math id="S3.p18.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup\Sigma^{\prime}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊔</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}\sqcup\ldots\sqcup\Sigma^{\prime}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary
decomposition as required in Definition <a href="#Thmdfn7" title="Definition 7. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Let <math id="S3.p18.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha\,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha\,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> be a transposition in <math id="S3.p18.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the argument above shows
that <math id="S3.p18.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p18.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows
that
<math id="S3.p18.m33" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=(\alpha\,\beta)^{G}\subseteq G^{\prime}=G^{\prime}_{1}\times\cdots\times G^{\prime}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(\alpha\,\beta)^{G}\subseteq G^{\prime}=G^{\prime}_{1}\times\cdots\times G^{\prime}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p18.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}_{j}=G\cap S(\Sigma^{\prime}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}_{j}=G\cap S(\Sigma^{\prime}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus each orbit <math id="S3.p18.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.p18.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\langle T\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\langle T\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in some orbit <math id="S3.p18.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assuming <math id="S3.p18.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive subgroup of <math id="S3.p18.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma^{\prime}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma^{\prime}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> we conclude that
<math id="S3.p18.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}=\Sigma^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}=\Sigma^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the uniqueness statement. The other
properties were already proved.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a covering of smooth, irreducible, projective
curves of degree <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that at least one of the discriminant points
is simple and suppose that the monodromy group of the covering is <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a unique decomposition
<math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\overset{\pi_{1}}{\longrightarrow}\tilde{Y}\overset{\pi_{2}}{\longrightarrow}Y,\;\pi=\pi_{2}\circ\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\overset{\pi_{1}}{\longrightarrow}\tilde{Y}\overset{\pi_{2}}{\longrightarrow}Y,\;\pi=\pi_{2}\circ\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=\deg\pi_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=\deg\pi_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}:X\to\tilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}:X\to\tilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> has primitive monodromy group. If
<math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>
has <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> simple discriminant points so does <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}:X\to\tilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}:X\to\tilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a bijective correspondence between these two sets.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">One chooses an unramified point <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and a bijection <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\pi^{-1}(b_{0})\overset{~}{\longrightarrow}\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\pi^{-1}(b_{0})\overset{~}{\longrightarrow}\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. One applies
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 3.12. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a> to the monodromy group of <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> and obtains a decomposition <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\tilde{Y}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\tilde{Y}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Replacing <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> by another bijection results in replacing the
monodromy group <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by a conjugate one <math id="S3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=s^{-1}Gs" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=s^{-1}Gs</annotation></semantics></math>. The conjugation
by <math id="S3.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms the <math id="S3.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of transpositions contained in
<math id="S3.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of transpositions contained in <math id="S3.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
<math id="S3.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> transforms the corresponding decompositions of <math id="S3.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}_{i}={\Sigma_{i}}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}_{i}={\Sigma_{i}}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the uniqueness
of the decomposition <math id="S3.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\tilde{Y}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\tilde{Y}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> with the required
properties. The last statement of the corollary is obvious.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, projective curve of genus <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\;2)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime then
<math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not prime
let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be its maximal divisor <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2d^{\prime}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2d^{\prime}-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the connected
components of <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which correspond to simple coverings
with monodromy groups <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are in one-to-one correspondence with
the equivalence classes of unramified coverings <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\tilde{Y}\to Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tilde{Y}\to Y]</annotation></semantics></math>
of degrees <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}|d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}|d</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}\neq 1,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}\neq 1,d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime every transitive subgroup
of <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive, so
<math id="S3.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S3.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not
prime.
Let <math id="S3.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple covering of degree <math id="S3.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, simply ramified
in <math id="S3.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points, with monodromy group <math id="S3.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the decomposition
<math id="S3.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\overset{\pi_{1}}{\longrightarrow}\tilde{Y}\overset{\pi_{2}}{\longrightarrow}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\overset{\pi_{1}}{\longrightarrow}\tilde{Y}\overset{\pi_{2}}{\longrightarrow}Y</annotation></semantics></math> of
Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 3.13. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a> is with étale <math id="S3.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore by the
uniqueness of this decomposition if <math id="S3.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an equivalence
of coverings of <math id="S3.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by an equivalence
<math id="S3.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\tilde{Y}\to\tilde{Y}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\tilde{Y}\to\tilde{Y}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given an étale covering
<math id="S3.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}:\tilde{Y}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}:\tilde{Y}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S3.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}\neq 1,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}\neq 1,d</annotation></semantics></math> we consider the Hurwitz
space <math id="S3.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d_{1},n}(\tilde{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d_{1},n}(\tilde{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=d/d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=d/d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is
connected by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. We then consider the Zariski open
subset consisting of <math id="S3.p20.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[X\to\tilde{Y}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X\to\tilde{Y}]</annotation></semantics></math> with discriminant points
belonging to different fibers of <math id="S3.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}:\tilde{Y}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}:\tilde{Y}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Every such
<math id="S3.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to\tilde{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to\tilde{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a simple covering of <math id="S3.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way one
obtains a connected component of <math id="S3.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> whose
conjugacy class of monodromy groups is imprimitive. Vice versa by
Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 3.13. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a> every connected component of
<math id="S3.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> whose associated conjugacy class of monodromy
groups is imprimitive is obtained in this way.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmexax1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exa">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexax1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example</span></span><span id="Thmexax1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexax1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve of genus <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The connected
unramified coverings of <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of degree 2 are classified up to
equivalence by the points of order 2 in the Jacobian <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Theorem 3.14. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a> we obtain if <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> there are
<math id="Thmexax1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2g}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2g}-1</annotation></semantics></math> different connected components of
<math id="Thmexax1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which parametrize coverings with imprimitive
monodromy group. The monodromy group in this case is isomorphic to the
dihedral group <math id="Thmexax1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The case <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2d-4,\quad g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2d-4,\quad g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless otherwise specified in this section we shall work with Hurwitz
systems <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\linebreak\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\linebreak\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2,\;g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2,\;g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are transpositions in
<math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of transpositions in <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that the subgroup
<math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle T\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle T\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
has only one
nontrivial domain of transitivity
<math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\#\,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\#\,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We have the inequality <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq e-1,\;n+|s|\geq 2(e-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq e-1,\;n+|s|\geq 2(e-1)</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>) so if <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\#\,\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\#\,\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is
fixed the minimum for <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is reached for <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=e-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=e-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|=e-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|=e-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This
happens if and only if <math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle of order
<math id="S4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9</span></span><span id="Thmdfn9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call a sequence of transpositions <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
<em id="Thmdfn9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimal</em> if
<math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle of order <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of transpositions of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle T\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle T\rangle=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique
nontrivial domain of transitivity <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is
not minimal. Then for any <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a
braid-equivalent sequence <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}=(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{n-1}=t^{\prime}_{n}=(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma,\;b\neq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma,\;b\neq a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Without loss of generality we may assume that <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
domains of transitivity of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a
braid-equivalent sequence we may assume it has the normal form of
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. The non-minimality hypothesis <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;|s|</annotation></semantics></math> implies
that <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-|s|)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-|s|)/2</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of the form <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1}2_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1}2_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1}1_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1}1_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq q</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the
cyclic group <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{t_{1}}^{u}\cdots{t_{n}}^{u}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{t_{1}}^{u}\cdots{t_{n}}^{u}=s</annotation></semantics></math>, so the sequence
<math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({t_{1}}^{u},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({t_{1}}^{u},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{u})</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to
<math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo</a>]</cite>). If <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math> we may find
<math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1})^{u}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1})^{u}=a</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we let
<math id="S4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=(2_{1})^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=(2_{1})^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we find <math id="S4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1})^{u}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1})^{u}=a</annotation></semantics></math> and we let <math id="S4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=(1_{2})^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=(1_{2})^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> we find <math id="S4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\langle s\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\langle s\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{i})^{u}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{i})^{u}=a</annotation></semantics></math> and we
let <math id="S4.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=(1_{1})^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=(1_{1})^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. In each case the sequence
<math id="S4.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({t_{1}}^{u},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({t_{1}}^{u},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{u})</annotation></semantics></math> contains the adjacent pair <math id="S4.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab),(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab),(ab)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moving it to the end we obtain the sequence required in the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_block">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Hurwitz system
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i},\lambda_{k},\mu_{k}\in S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> let
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
domains of transitivity of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
ordered in such a way that
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})\ =\ (\#\,\Sigma_{1},\#\,\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\#\,\Sigma_{m},0,0,\ldots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})\ =\ (\#\,\Sigma_{1},\#\,\Sigma_{2},\ldots,\#\,\Sigma_{m},0,0,\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be the associated partition of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
subsequence of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> composed of those transpositions
which move points of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=\#\,T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=\#\,T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call
<math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> minimal or non-minimal if the sequence <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
minimal or non-minimal respectively. The trivial case <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1,\;T_{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1,\;T_{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed minimal.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> be a
Hurwitz system with monodromy group <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the domains of
transitivity of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> ordered as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="4.2. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. Suppose that
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal in lexicographic order among the
partitions associated with all Hurwitz systems of the type
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
braid-equivalent to the given one. Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive) if and only if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|=2|\underline{\Sigma}|=2(d-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|=2|\underline{\Sigma}|=2(d-m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> the
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, equivalently <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=|s|_{\Sigma_{i}}|=\#\,\Sigma_{i}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=|s|_{\Sigma_{i}}|=\#\,\Sigma_{i}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has a
single nontrivial domain of transitivity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We proved in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> that if <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive then <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. That the maximality
of <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> imply that
<math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fact evident from the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> (cf. Step <a href="#Thmstepb1" title="Step 1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We shall further assume that
<math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not transitive. Suppose, by way of
contradiction, that there is a non-minimal <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}=\Sigma_{k+1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}=\Sigma_{k+1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k}\cup\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}=\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k}\cup\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}=\langle T_{k}\rangle,\&gt;H_{2}=\langle T_{1},\ldots,\widehat{T_{k}},\ldots,T_{m},\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}=\langle T_{k}\rangle,\&gt;H_{2}=\langle T_{1},\ldots,\widehat{T_{k}},\ldots,T_{m},\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle H_{1},H_{2}\rangle=G=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle H_{1},H_{2}\rangle=G=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> one has
<math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{G}=\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{G}=\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in G=\langle H_{1},H_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in G=\langle H_{1},H_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> be chosen in such a way
that <math id="S4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{h}\in\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h}\in\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and furthermore the
length of the factorization
<math id="S4.p3.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h=h_{1}h_{2}\cdots h_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=h_{1}h_{2}\cdots h_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal possible
among all <math id="S4.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S4.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S4.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{g}\in\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{g}\in\Sigma_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\Sigma_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim <math id="S4.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact by minimality we have <math id="S4.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}\in H_{2},\&gt;h_{r}\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}\in H_{2},\&gt;h_{r}\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and for
each <math id="S4.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,r-1</annotation></semantics></math> the adjacent <math id="S4.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i},h_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i},h_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> do not belong to the
same subgroup <math id="S4.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S4.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by minimality
<math id="S4.p3.m33" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma_{k}\cup\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma_{k}\cup\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S4.p3.m34" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> we might replace <math id="S4.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S4.p3.m36" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{\prime}=a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}=a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p3.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}\in\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
group <math id="S4.p3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S4.p3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, therefore since <math id="S4.p3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{r-1}\in H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{r-1}\in H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.p3.m41" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}=a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-1}}=a^{h_{1}\cdots h_{r-2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S4.p3.m42" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a^{h}=a^{h_{1}\cdots(h_{r-2}h_{r})}=a^{h^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h}=a^{h_{1}\cdots(h_{r-2}h_{r})}=a^{h^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p3.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has a shorter
factorization then <math id="S4.p3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an absurd, so <math id="S4.p3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We have thus
proved there exists an <math id="S4.p3.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p3.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{h}\in\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{h}\in\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.p3.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>
we may replace <math id="S4.p3.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent sequence which contains the
adjacent pair <math id="S4.p3.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab),(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab),(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.p3.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Main Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Main Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> replacing <math id="S4.p3.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab),(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab),(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p3.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)^{h},(ab)^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)^{h},(ab)^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> one
obtains a braid-equivalent Hurwitz system. The following cases may
occur.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcasea1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_casea">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcasea1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 1</span></span><span id="Thmcasea1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcasea1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcasea1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmcasea1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{h}\in\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{h}\in\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i,\;1\leq j\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i,\;1\leq j\leq m</annotation></semantics></math></em>. If
<math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> then the new Hurwitz system has <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}\cup\Sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}\cup\Sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
domain of transitivity, so the corresponding partition
Eq.(<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.2. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) is greater than <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> in
lexicographical order. If <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math> we interchange <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and make
the same conclusion. In both cases we obtain a contradiction with the
maximality of <math id="Thmcasea1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmcasea2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_casea">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcasea2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case 2</span></span><span id="Thmcasea2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcasea2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcasea2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{h}\in\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{h}\in\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)^{h}=(xy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)^{h}=(xy)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moving the pair
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(xy),(xy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(xy),(xy)</annotation></semantics></math> to the right of <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a braid-equivalent sequence
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{T}_{i}=T_{i},(xy),(xy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{T}_{i}=T_{i},(xy),(xy)</annotation></semantics></math> with the same domain of transitivity as
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Canceling the pair from the obtained Hurwitz system does not
change the monodromy group <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us choose <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{g}\in\Sigma_{i},\;y^{g}\notin\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{g}\in\Sigma_{i},\;y^{g}\notin\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Main
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Main Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we obtain a new Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{t}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{t}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> whose associated partition <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> has the
property that its first <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> terms are greater in lexicographical order
then the first <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> terms of <math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>. This
contradicts the maximality of the partition
<math id="Thmcasea2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The claim that <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal for every <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is proved. According to
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> we have
<math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|\geq 2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|\geq 2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and if this inequality
is strict we can apply the argument of
Step <a href="#Thmstepb1" title="Step 1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> in order to increase
<math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> which is impossible . Thus
<math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally if <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s|_{\Sigma_{i}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s|_{\Sigma_{i}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so minimality of <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the maximality of <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>
implies that only <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may be nontrivial domain of transitivity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_block">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Hurwitz system
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> we let
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\lambda}=(\lambda_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\lambda}=(\lambda_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mu}=(\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mu}=(\mu_{1},\ldots,\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\lambda}|=(|\lambda_{1}|,\ldots,|\lambda_{g}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\lambda}|=(|\lambda_{1}|,\ldots,|\lambda_{g}|)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\underline{\mu}|=(|\mu_{1}|,\ldots,|\mu_{g}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\underline{\mu}|=(|\mu_{1}|,\ldots,|\mu_{g}|)</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="3.7. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>). We
consider the class <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of Hurwitz systems braid-equivalent to the
given one. When dealing with the problem of finding a system in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of
a simplest form it makes sense to assume (without loss of generality) that
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
the following property:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(*) <em id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal in
lexicographic order among the Hurwitz systems in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and furthermore
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="4.2. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) is maximal in
lexicographic order among the Hurwitz systems in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of the type
<math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
</em></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3,\;n=2d-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3,\;n=2d-4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, projective curve of
genus <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Hurwitz space <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>
parametrizing simple coverings with monodromy groups equal to
<math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> it suffices to show that the equivalence
class
(modulo inner automorphisms) of every Hurwitz system
<math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with
monodromy group <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> may be reduced by a finite number of braid moves
to the normal form</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,1,(1d)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,1,(1d)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where each <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1i)</annotation></semantics></math> appears twice for <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of
generality we may assume that
<math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal
<math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math> and maximal
<math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> (*)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepc1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepc">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepc1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1</span></span><span id="Thmstepc1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepc1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepc1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepc1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We claim <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></em> Let us first
suppose that <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> be a transposition such that
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(ab)\lambda_{1}|&lt;|\lambda_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(ab)\lambda_{1}|&lt;|\lambda_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math>. First suppose that <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we
have <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a transitive group, so by
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we can replace <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a
braid-equivalent sequence <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}=(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}=(ab)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Eq.(<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)) one transforms
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts the minimality
of <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
according to Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> the subgroup <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has two orbits: <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}=d-1,\;\#\,\Sigma_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}=d-1,\;\#\,\Sigma_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover replacing if necessary
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent sequence we may assume that
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=t_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=t_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv 1(mod\,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv 1(mod\,2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Varying over all Hurwitz
systems with <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed and
braid-equivalent to the given one we want to figure out which transpositions
may appear at the first place. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\},\;\Sigma_{d}=\{a_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\},\;\Sigma_{d}=\{a_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha,\beta\}\subseteq\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> then by
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> one may replace the sequence
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent one
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((a_{\alpha},a_{\beta}),\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((a_{\alpha},a_{\beta}),\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}=\langle\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}=\langle\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\langle G_{1},G_{2}\rangle=S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\langle G_{1},G_{2}\rangle=S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists h\in G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists h\in G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}\not\subseteq\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>⊈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}\not\subseteq\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}=\{1,\ldots,d\}-\{a_{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}=\{1,\ldots,d\}-\{a_{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{d}}^{h}=a_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{d}}^{h}=a_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. By Main
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Main Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> the Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({t_{1}}^{h},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{h};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({t_{1}}^{h},\ldots,{t_{n}}^{h};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>
is braid-equivalent to the given one. Applying again Mochizuki’s
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we see that every <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{\alpha}b_{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{\alpha}b_{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)\neq(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)\neq(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> may be placed first in some
braid-equivalent Hurwitz system. We may vary <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}\not\subseteq\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>⊈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{1}}^{h}\not\subseteq\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, or equivalently with the property
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{d}}^{h}\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{d}}^{h}\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the orbit <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{d}}^{G_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{d}}^{G_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>
elements, then an arbitrary transposition <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> may be placed first
in some braid-equivalent Hurwitz system
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Doing
this for <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> and applying the braid move <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime\prime}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> we
transform
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> thus obtaining a contradiction
with the minimality of <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. It
remains to consider the case <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,{a_{d}}^{G_{2}}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,{a_{d}}^{G_{2}}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=(a_{c}a_{d})\lambda_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=(a_{c}a_{d})\lambda_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}^{\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}^{\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}^{\prime}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}^{\prime}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we might decrease
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> transforming <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with
an appropriate <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> as we saw above and this contradicts the
minimality of <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains
to consider the case <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=(a_{c}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=(a_{c}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\{1,\ldots,d-1\},\&gt;e\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\{1,\ldots,d-1\},\&gt;e\neq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing the <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent one we may assume that it equals
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((a_{c}a_{e}),(a_{c}a_{e}),\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((a_{c}a_{e}),(a_{c}a_{e}),\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{11}^{\prime\prime}:t_{1}\mapsto\lambda_{1}t_{1}\lambda_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{11}^{\prime\prime}:t_{1}\mapsto\lambda_{1}t_{1}\lambda_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying
this braid move we obtain
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((a_{e}a_{d}),(a_{c}a_{e}),\ldots;\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}^{\prime},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((a_{e}a_{d}),(a_{c}a_{e}),\ldots;\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}^{\prime},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. We may
replace <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m73" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{e}a_{d}),(a_{c}a_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{e}a_{d}),(a_{c}a_{e})</annotation></semantics></math> by the braid-equivalent pair
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m74" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{c}a_{d}),(a_{e}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{c}a_{d}),(a_{e}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and then apply <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m75" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> transforming
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m76" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m77" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{c}a_{d})\cdot(a_{c}a_{d})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{c}a_{d})\cdot(a_{c}a_{d})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts
again the minimality of <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m78" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This proves <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m79" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m80" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{k-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{k-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m81" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m82" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{k-1}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{k-1},\mu_{k-1}]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k-1}=[\lambda_{1},\mu_{1}]\cdots[\lambda_{k-1},\mu_{k-1}]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The
braid move <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m83" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m84" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m85" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{-1}\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{-1}\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> according to Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> so the same
argument as above may be applied proving that <math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m86" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>
contradicts the minimality of
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m87" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
<math id="Thmstepc1.p1.m88" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepc2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepc">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepc2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2</span></span><span id="Thmstepc2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepc2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepc2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepc2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We claim the group <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has
orbits <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}=\{a_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}=\{a_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and there
exists <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[1,d-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[1,d-1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that every <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> equals either 1 or
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{c}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{c}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math></em>. For every Hurwitz system with <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1</annotation></semantics></math> we have by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> (b) that <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
transforms <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{-1}\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{-1}\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}=\cdots=\mu_{k-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}=\cdots=\mu_{k-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Upon a substitution <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\leftrightarrow\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\leftrightarrow\rho</annotation></semantics></math>
we may use arguments similar to
those of Step <a href="#Thmstepc1" title="Step 1. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> trying to decrease <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{k}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{k}|</annotation></semantics></math> and thus
obtaining a contradiction with the minimality of
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. We are not allowed however
to use braid moves of the types <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ik}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ik}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ik}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> since
these change <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> while we want to preserve at every braid
move the equality <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We thus have the following: <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
has orbits <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}=\{a_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}=\{a_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=(a_{c}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=(a_{c}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}=\langle\mu_{k},\ldots,\mu_{g}\rangle\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}=\langle\mu_{k},\ldots,\mu_{g}\rangle\subseteq S(\{a_{c},a_{d}\})\times S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here if <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math> we have
either <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}=\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}=(a_{c}a_{d})\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}=(a_{c}a_{d})\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}\in S(\Sigma_{1}-\{a_{c}\})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}^{\prime\prime}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> respectively we might apply Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and the braid move
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1\ell}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1\ell}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to decrease <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{\ell}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{\ell}|</annotation></semantics></math>. This is
impossible by the minimality of
<math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math> so for every <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>
either <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmstepc2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}=(a_{c}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}=(a_{c}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmstepc3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_stepc">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmstepc3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 3</span></span><span id="Thmstepc3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmstepc3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmstepc3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Thmstepc3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">We claim
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\ell&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\ell&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math>,  <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}=(a_{c}a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}=(a_{c}a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>
</em>. Since renumbering and braid moves are commutative operations we may
assume without loss of generality that <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{1,\ldots,d-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{1,\ldots,d-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math> so every <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> equals either 1 or <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be the
minimal index such that <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}=(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}=(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=g</annotation></semantics></math> there is nothing to
prove, so suppose that <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing eventually <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by a
braid-equivalent sequence we may assume that
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda_{1},\mu_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{g},\mu_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> has the
following form:</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,\underset{k}{1,(1d)},\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑘</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,\underset{k}{1,(1d)},\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmstepc3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">First suppose that <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that replacing <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>
one obtains a braid-equivalent Hurwitz system. Indeed <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
(cf. Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm9" title="Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a>) transforms Eq.(<a href="#S4.E15" title="In Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) into
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((2d),(12),\ldots;\linebreak\ldots,(2d),(1d),\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((2d),(12),\ldots;\linebreak\ldots,(2d),(1d),\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Apply the following
sequence of braid-equivalences: replace <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2d),(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2d),(12)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(12),(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(12),(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>;
move this pair to the end by braid moves of type <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; perform
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; move the pair <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(12),(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(12),(1d)</annotation></semantics></math> backward to the front by
braid moves of type <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; replace it by <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2d),(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2d),(12)</annotation></semantics></math> and finally
apply <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1k}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. One obtains</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,\underset{k}{1,(1d)},\ldots,1,(1d))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑘</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-1)(1\,d-1);1,1,\ldots,\underset{k}{1,(1d)},\ldots,1,(1d))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmstepc3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">as claimed. We may thus suppose that Eq.(<a href="#S4.E15" title="In Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) equals
Eq.(<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). Applying <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to Eq.(<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) and
replacing <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2d),(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2d),(12)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d),(2d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d),(2d)</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1d),(2d),(13),(13),\ldots;\ldots,1,(1d),\ldots,(2d),(1d))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1d),(2d),(13),(13),\ldots;\ldots,1,(1d),\ldots,(2d),(1d))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmstepc3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Applying <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1k}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1k}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> one transforms <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}^{\prime}=(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}^{\prime}=(1d)</annotation></semantics></math> into 1 without
changing <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}=(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}=(1d)</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d),(2d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d),(2d)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2d),(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2d),(12)</annotation></semantics></math> and applying
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1g}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1g}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a Hurwitz system with <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}^{\prime}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}^{\prime}=\cdots=\lambda_{g}^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}^{\prime}=\cdots=\mu_{k}^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}^{\prime}=\cdots=\mu_{k}^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\ell}^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1d)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that the assumption <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{k}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{k}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math> contradicts the minimality of
<math id="Thmstepc3.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\underline{\lambda}|,|\underline{\mu}|)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have so far worked with Hurwitz systems. When working with
equivalence classes we may moreover renumber arbitrarily <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We thus conclude that the equivalence class (modulo inner
automorphisms) of a Hurwitz system of the type of Step <a href="#Thmstepc3" title="Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is
braid-equivalent to the equivalence class Eq.(<a href="#S4.E15" title="In Step 3. ‣ Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) with
<math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=g</annotation></semantics></math>, so it has the normal form Eq.(<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) as claimed
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>The case <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2d-6,\&gt;g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2d-6,\&gt;g=1</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless otherwise specified in this section we shall work with Hurwitz
systems <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are transpositions in <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_pro">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, be a Hurwitz
system with monodromy group <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the equivalence class
<math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to</span></p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(1\,e+1\ldots d)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(1\,e+1\ldots d)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may assume without loss of generality that <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> is
minimal and <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal in lexicographic
order as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> (*). By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> the subgroup
<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle\subseteq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle\subseteq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> has a single
nontrivial domain of transitivity <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and furthermore since
<math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}\cdots t_{n}=1</annotation></semantics></math> we may assume that <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=t_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=t_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv 1(mod\,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv 1(mod\,2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Decomposing <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> into
a product of independent cycles <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> we have
each <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most one element of <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed,
otherwise using Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and applying the braid
move <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ng}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Eq.(<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)) we might decrease <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore the transitivity of <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\mu\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\mu\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> implies that every <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains exactly one element of
<math id="S5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and each element of <math id="S5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Sigma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Sigma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in
one of <math id="S5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality <math id="S5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalent to <math id="S5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;d-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;d-e</annotation></semantics></math>,
implies that at least one element of <math id="S5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> does not appear in the
cycles <math id="S5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i},\;1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i},\;1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that if <math id="S5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then there is a braid-equivalent Hurwitz system
<math id="S5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};1,\mu^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,t^{\prime}_{n};1,\mu^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle of order
<math id="S5.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-e+1</annotation></semantics></math> containing a single element of <math id="S5.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Braid moves and
renumbering of <math id="S5.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> are commutative operations, so for
proving the claim we may assume without loss of generality that
<math id="S5.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};1,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};1,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> equals</span></p>
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(2\;e+1\ldots e+i_{1})(3\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(2\;e+1\ldots e+i_{1})(3\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Performing <math id="S5.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1\,e+1),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);(1\,e+1),(2\,e+1\ldots)(3\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1\,e+1),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);(1\,e+1),(2\,e+1\ldots)(3\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Moving the pair <math id="S5.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\,e+1),(12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\,e+1),(12)</annotation></semantics></math> to the end, replacing it by
<math id="S5.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(12),(2\,e+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(12),(2\,e+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and performing <math id="S5.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e),(12),(12);(1\,e+1),(2\,e+2\ldots)(3\,e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e),(12),(12);(1\,e+1),(2\,e+2\ldots)(3\,e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Let us consider the system</span></p>
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(2\;e+2\ldots e+i_{1})(3\;e+1\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1e),(1e);1,(2\;e+2\ldots e+i_{1})(3\;e+1\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Moving the pair <math id="S5.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13),(13)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13),(13)</annotation></semantics></math> to the front and then performing
<math id="S5.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1\,e+1),(13),(12),(12),(14),(14),\ldots;(1\,e+1),(2\,e+2\ldots)(3\,e+1\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1\,e+1),(13),(12),(12),(14),(14),\ldots;(1\,e+1),(2\,e+2\ldots)(3\,e+1\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">Moving <math id="S5.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\,e+1),(13)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\,e+1),(13)</annotation></semantics></math> to the end, replacing it by <math id="S5.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13),(3\,e+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13),(3\,e+1)</annotation></semantics></math>
and performing <math id="S5.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((12),(12),(14),\ldots,(1e),(13),(13);(1\;e+1),(2\;e+2\ldots)(3\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((12),(12),(14),\ldots,(1e),(13),(13);(1\;e+1),(2\;e+2\ldots)(3\;e+i_{1}+1\ldots)\ldots).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.7.1" class="ltx_text">Since Eq.(<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) is braid-equivalent to Eq.(<a href="#S5.E21" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) and
Eq.(<a href="#S5.E21" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) is braid-equivalent to Eq.(<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) we
conclude that Eq.(<a href="#S5.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) is braid-equivalent to Eq.(<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>).
The sequence Eq.(<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) is obtained from Eq.(<a href="#S5.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) by
moving one element from the cycle <math id="S5.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the cycle <math id="S5.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Repeating
this transformation several times we obtain a cycle <math id="S5.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S5.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-e+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We thus proved that
replacing the given Hurwitz system by a braid-equivalent one we may
assume that <math id="S5.p2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has a single nontrivial
orbit <math id="S5.p2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{e}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},\ldots,a_{e}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{i}a_{e+1}\ldots a_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{i}a_{e+1}\ldots a_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.p2.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{d}\}=\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{d}\}=\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Renumbering and normalizing the sequence
<math id="S5.p2.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> according to the classical result of Clebsch and
Hurwitz we obtain that <math id="S5.p2.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};1,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};1,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to
the equivalence class Eq.(<a href="#S5.E17" title="In Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmrem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> may be easily adapted in order to
show that every equivalence class
<math id="Thmrem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with full monodromy group <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to</p>
<table id="S5.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(12),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);(1\,e+1\ldots d),1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(12),(12),\ldots,(1e),(1e);(1\,e+1\ldots d),1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmrem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here one should modify Condition <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>(*) assuming
without loss of generality <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)=(|\lambda|,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)=(|\lambda|,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal
in the reverse lexicographical order and <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is
maximal in lexicographical order. One then repeats the proof of the
proposition using <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmrem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hurwitz system with monodromy
group <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the domains of transitivity of <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
ordered in such a way that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal and
<math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>(*).
Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the following three conditions cannot hold simultaneously.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">i.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is non-minimal (cf. </span><a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="4.2. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">ii.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (equivalently there exists </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\lambda^{-1}}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\lambda^{-1}}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">iii.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\lambda}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\lambda}=b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Decomposing <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> into a product of independent cycles
<math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}\cdots\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}\cdots\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> we see that each of the two
conditions of (ii) is equivalent to the existence of a <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
which contains elements of both <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\{1,\ldots,d\}-\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\{1,\ldots,d\}-\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> be the decomposition of <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> into a product of
independent cycles. The minimality of <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that neither
<math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> may contain two different elements of some
<math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact if this were the case then we might apply
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and one of the braid moves
<math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to decrease
<math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular for each <math id="S5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we have an
alternative: either
<math id="S5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\lambda}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\lambda}=x</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose, by way of contradiction, that (i), (ii) and (iii) hold simultaneously.
Let <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=[\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> we must have <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> since otherwise
<math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive, so applying
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and the braid move <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we
might decrease <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> which contradicts the minimality of
<math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> assumed in the lemma. Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> yields
moreover that <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+|s|_{\Sigma_{1}}|=2(\#\,\Sigma_{1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, so
Condition (i) means <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s|_{\Sigma_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s|_{\Sigma_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>
domains of transitivity <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\ldots,\Delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim one
can choose <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying (ii) and (iii) respectively so that
they belong to different domains. In fact if <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and all
<math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to
<math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> then we may replace <math id="S5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> by an arbitrary element of some
other <math id="S5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the alternative of the preceding
paragraph. Let <math id="S5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Delta_{i},\;b\in\Delta_{j},\;i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Delta_{i},\;b\in\Delta_{j},\;i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subsequence of <math id="S5.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
consisting of the transpositions that move elements of <math id="S5.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}=a^{s},\;b^{\prime}=b^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}=a^{s},\;b^{\prime}=b^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 3.6. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> we can replace
<math id="S5.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by a sequence <math id="S5.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdots,(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}),(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdots,(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}),(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> and then move the pair
<math id="S5.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}),(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\prime}b^{\prime}),(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to the end of <math id="S5.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. We then perform the braid move
<math id="S5.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which transforms <math id="S5.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}=(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}=(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S5.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})^{s^{-1}\lambda}=(ab)^{\lambda}=(a^{\lambda}b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\prime}b^{\prime})^{s^{-1}\lambda}=(ab)^{\lambda}=(a^{\lambda}b)</annotation></semantics></math>. The group
generated by the new sequence <math id="S5.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle T^{\prime}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle T^{\prime}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has orbit
<math id="S5.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}\cup\{a^{\lambda}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}\cup\{a^{\lambda}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> one
may replace <math id="S5.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((a\,a^{\lambda}),\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((a\,a^{\lambda}),\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Performing
<math id="S5.p4.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> one transforms <math id="S5.p4.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p4.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a\,a^{\lambda})\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a\,a^{\lambda})\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
and clearly <math id="S5.p4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(a\,a^{\lambda})\lambda|&lt;|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(a\,a^{\lambda})\lambda|&lt;|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts
the minimality of <math id="S5.p4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Hurwitz system with monodromy
group <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> is
minimal and <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal as in
<a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>(*). Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the equivalence class <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> is
braid-equivalent to Eq.(<a href="#S5.E17" title="In Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the orbits of
<math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> we have <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for each
<math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}=e</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose, by way of contradiction, that
<math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> its conditions (i) – (iii)
cannot hold simultaneously. The orbit <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-minimal since
<math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s|_{\Sigma_{1}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s|_{\Sigma_{1}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As we saw in the course of the proof of
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> the minimality of
<math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that if one decomposes <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> into a
product of independent cycles <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\prod\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\prod\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> then every
<math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most one element of <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality
<math id="S5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq d-1</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}=e&gt;d-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}=e&gt;d-e</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there exists an
element <math id="S5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\lambda}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\lambda}=b</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
Condition (ii) of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> must fail to hold, i.e. we have
<math id="S5.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\lambda}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\lambda}=a</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of fixed points of <math id="S5.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S5.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\supset\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\supset\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> so the group <math id="S5.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> leaves
<math id="S5.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> invariant. Since <math id="S5.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\mu}=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\mu}=F</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore the assumption <math id="S5.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S5.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\lambda,\mu\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n},\lambda,\mu\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is not a transitive group which is a
contradiction. The last part of the lemma refers to
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmrem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This lemma may be stated differently: if <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmrem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> then one can decrease <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math> by a sequence of
braid moves (at the expense of possible increasing of <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|</annotation></semantics></math>).
Modifying appropriately Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> one can prove similarly that
given a Hurwitz system <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> with full
monodromy group <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1\leq n&lt;2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmrem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> imply that one can decrease <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|</annotation></semantics></math> by a
sequence of braid moves (at the expense of possible increasing of
<math id="Thmrem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4,\;n=2d-6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4,\;n=2d-6</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an elliptic curve. Then the
Hurwitz space <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing simple coverings
with monodromy groups equal to <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 1.10. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> it suffices to show that given a Hurwitz
system <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> with monodromy group <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> its
equivalence class is braid-equivalent to</span></p>
<table id="S5.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-2),(1\,d-2);1,(1\,d-1\,d)]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(12),(12),(13),(13),\ldots,(1\,d-2),(1\,d-2);1,(1\,d-1\,d)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Without loss of generality we may assume that
<math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> and
maximal <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>(*). Here
<math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\ldots,\Sigma_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are the domains of
transitivity of <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> ordered in
such a way that
<math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\#\,\Sigma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. We aim to prove that <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> in order
to apply Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Let <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=[\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math>. Our first
goal is to prove <math id="S5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose, by way of contradiction, that <math id="S5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so
in particular <math id="S5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math>
is an even permutation we have <math id="S5.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|\equiv 0(mod\&gt;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|\equiv 0(mod\&gt;2)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>
then <math id="S5.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+|s|\geq 2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+|s|\geq 2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, so by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> the group
<math id="S5.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}=\langle t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive. Using
Mochizuki’s Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and the braid move <math id="S5.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we can
transform <math id="S5.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda^{\prime}|&lt;|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda^{\prime}|&lt;|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>. This
contradicts the minimality of <math id="S5.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if <math id="S5.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>
then <math id="S5.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|=2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> the group <math id="S5.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has two domains
of transitivity <math id="S5.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal. If <math id="S5.p6.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}\cdots\lambda_{k},\;\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}\cdots\lambda_{k},\;\mu=\mu_{1}\cdots\mu_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are the factorizations into
independent cycles, then as we saw in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>
the minimality of <math id="S5.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that each <math id="S5.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S5.p6.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a transposition of the type <math id="S5.p6.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ac)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ac)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p6.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{1},\;c\in\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{1},\;c\in\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clear that <math id="S5.p6.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{k,\ell\}\leq\#\,\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{k,\ell\}\leq\#\,\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p6.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=(s|_{\Sigma_{1}})^{\sim},\;s_{2}=(s|_{\Sigma_{2}})^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=(s|_{\Sigma_{1}})^{\sim},\;s_{2}=(s|_{\Sigma_{2}})^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<sup id="S5.p6.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∼</span></sup>
means trivial extension from <math id="S5.p6.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\Sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\Sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p6.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
<math id="S5.p6.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=s_{1}s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=s_{1}s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the following cases may occur: a) <math id="S5.p6.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{1}|=2,\;|s_{2}|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{1}|=2,\;|s_{2}|=0</annotation></semantics></math>; 
b)  <math id="S5.p6.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{1}|=1,\;|s_{2}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{1}|=1,\;|s_{2}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>;  c)  <math id="S5.p6.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{1}|=0,\;|s_{2}|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{1}|=0,\;|s_{2}|=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In Case (a) by minimality <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=(a_{1}c),\;\mu=(a_{2}c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=(a_{1}c),\;\mu=(a_{2}c)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c\}=\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c\}=\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\neq a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\neq a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> since
<math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=(a_{2}a_{1}c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=(a_{2}a_{1}c)</annotation></semantics></math>. This
contradicts <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}\in S(\Sigma_{1})\times S(\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=t_{1}\cdots t_{n}\in S(\Sigma_{1})\times S(\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, so Case (a) is impossible.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">In Case (b) we have <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math> by minimality and <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{1}\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-minimal and furthermore there exists an element <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\lambda}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\lambda}=b</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=(ac)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=(ac)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma_{1},\;a^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma_{1},\;a^{\lambda}\notin\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. It remains to consider the case <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},a_{2}\},\;\Sigma_{2}=\{c_{1},c_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},a_{2}\},\;\Sigma_{2}=\{c_{1},c_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a
transposition. We may assume that <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We exclude case by
case the possibilities for <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. The cases <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{1}c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{1}c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, or
<math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{2}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{2}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are impossible since
<math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The cases <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{2}c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{2}c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> are
impossible either since <math id="S5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=s_{1}s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=s_{1}s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a cycle of order
3. It remains to exclude the possibility <math id="S5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed here</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{1}c_{2})(a_{2}c_{1})=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">while <math id="S5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> must equal <math id="S5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1}a_{2})(c_{1}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1}a_{2})(c_{1}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may assume that <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> might be <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> only if
<math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{1}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(a_{2}c_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(a_{2}c_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The calculation above shows that in these
cases <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]\neq(a_{1}a_{2})(c_{1}c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]\neq(a_{1}a_{2})(c_{1}c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
So Case (b) is impossible.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">In Case (c) we have <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\,\Sigma_{2}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\,\Sigma_{2}=3</annotation></semantics></math> by minimality and
<math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s|_{\Sigma_{1}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s|_{\Sigma_{1}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\#\,\Sigma_{1}+\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\#\,\Sigma_{1}+\#\,\Sigma_{2}\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a contradiction with Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> by the same
argument we used in Case (b). The cases <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are excluded in the same way. It
remains to consider the case <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})(a_{3}c_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(a_{1}c_{1})(a_{2}c_{2})(a_{3}c_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S5.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\},\;\Sigma_{2}=\{c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\},\;\Sigma_{2}=\{c_{1},c_{2},c_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the subsequence of <math id="S5.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\ldots,t_{6})</annotation></semantics></math> which moves elements of
<math id="S5.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i},\;i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i},\;i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing <math id="S5.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by a braid-equivalent sequence we
may assume that <math id="S5.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T_{1}T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T_{1}T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=(a_{1}a_{2})(a_{1}a_{2})(a_{1}a_{3})(a_{1}a_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=(a_{1}a_{2})(a_{1}a_{2})(a_{1}a_{3})(a_{1}a_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Main Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Main Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 The main lemma ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> the Hurwitz system <math id="S5.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{1}T_{2};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{1}T_{2};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> is
braid-equivalent to <math id="S5.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({T_{1}}^{\mu}T_{2};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({T_{1}}^{\mu}T_{2};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S5.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#({\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}\cap\Sigma_{2})&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#({\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}\cap\Sigma_{2})&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math> then the group <math id="S5.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{T_{1}}^{\mu},T_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{T_{1}}^{\mu},T_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has a domain of transitivity
<math id="S5.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}={\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}\cup\Sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}={\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}\cup\Sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}&gt;\#\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\Sigma^{\prime}_{1}&gt;\#\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts the maximality of
<math id="S5.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\underline{\Sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\underline{\Sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}=\Sigma_{2},\&gt;{\Sigma_{2}}^{\mu}=\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma_{1}}^{\mu}=\Sigma_{2},\&gt;{\Sigma_{2}}^{\mu}=\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that the group
<math id="S5.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive which is excluded by
hypothesis. We thus excluded all possible cases with <math id="S5.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S5.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. Then from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> it follows that <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{1},\ldots,t_{n};\lambda,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> is braid-equivalent to Eq.(<a href="#S5.E23" title="In Proof. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>)
which proves the theorem when <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. So, the only case that
remains to be considered is <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4,n=2,[\lambda,\mu]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4,n=2,[\lambda,\mu]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we have
<math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\Sigma_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Sigma}=\{\Sigma_{1},\Sigma_{2},\Sigma_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#\Sigma_{1}=2,\;\#\,\Sigma_{2}=\#\,\Sigma_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#\Sigma_{1}=2,\;\#\,\Sigma_{2}=\#\,\Sigma_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\Sigma_{2}\cup\Sigma_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\Sigma_{2}\cup\Sigma_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose, by way of
contradiction, that <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Unless <math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(ac)(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(ac)(bd)</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}=\{a,b\},\;\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\{c,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}=\{a,b\},\;\overline{\Sigma}_{1}=\{c,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> we may apply the
argument of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and obtain a contradiction with the
minimality of <math id="S5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain a Hurwitz system of the
type <math id="S5.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with <math id="S5.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ac)(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ac)(bd)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The centralizer of <math id="S5.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(ac)(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(ac)(bd)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S5.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,(ac)(bd),(ad)(bc),(ab)(cd),(ac),(bd),(abcd),(adcb)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,(ac)(bd),(ad)(bc),(ab)(cd),(ac),(bd),(abcd),(adcb)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The braid
move <math id="S5.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{21}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{21}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms <math id="S5.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime\prime}=(ab)\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime\prime}=(ab)\mu</annotation></semantics></math> (cf.
Eq.(<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Corollary 1.9. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)). Thus the minimality of <math id="S5.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> excludes
the cases <math id="S5.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(ab)(cd),(abcd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(ab)(cd),(abcd)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(adcb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(adcb)</annotation></semantics></math>. The hypothesis of
primitivity of <math id="S5.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle t_{1},t_{2},\lambda,\mu\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle t_{1},t_{2},\lambda,\mu\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> excludes the cases
<math id="S5.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1,(ac)(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1,(ac)(bd)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ad)(bc)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ad)(bc)</annotation></semantics></math>. It thus remains to consider the cases
<math id="S5.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(ac)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(ac)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(bd)</annotation></semantics></math>. These are equivalent up to reordering, so it
suffices to consider <math id="S5.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=(ac)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=(ac)</annotation></semantics></math>. We perform the following sequence of
braid moves starting from <math id="S5.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying
<math id="S5.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain <math id="S5.p12.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((bc),(ab);(acdb),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((bc),(ab);(acdb),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing
<math id="S5.p12.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(bc),(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(bc),(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p12.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ac),(bc)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ac),(bc)</annotation></semantics></math> and then applying <math id="S5.p12.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf.
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 1.8. ‣ 1 Braid moves ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a> (f)) we obtain <math id="S5.p12.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ac)\mapsto(ac)^{(abc)}=(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ac)\mapsto(ac)^{(abc)}=(ab)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p12.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(acdb)\mapsto(ac)(acdb)=(adb)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(acdb)\mapsto(ac)(acdb)=(adb)</annotation></semantics></math>. So we obtain
<math id="S5.p12.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),(bc);(bad),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),(bc);(bad),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S5.p12.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{11}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> again we transform
<math id="S5.p12.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots;(bad),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots;(bad),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p12.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ldots;(bd),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ldots;(bd),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradicts the
minimality of <math id="S5.p12.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> of the initial Hurwitz system
<math id="S5.p12.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),(ac))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((ab),(ab);(ac)(bd),(ac))</annotation></semantics></math>. All possible cases with
<math id="S5.p12.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\lambda,\mu]=1,\;\lambda\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\lambda,\mu]=1,\;\lambda\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> were excluded, so <math id="S5.p12.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We
conclude that <math id="S5.p12.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},t_{2};\lambda,\mu)=((ab),(ab);1,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},t_{2};\lambda,\mu)=((ab),(ab);1,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimality of
<math id="S5.p12.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\lambda|,|\mu|)=(0,|\mu|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\lambda|,|\mu|)=(0,|\mu|)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that no independent cycle of the
factorization of <math id="S5.p12.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> may contain both <math id="S5.p12.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p12.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p12.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle(ab),\mu\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle(ab),\mu\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive we conclude that the factorization is either
<math id="S5.p12.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\mu_{1}\mu_{2}=(ac)(bd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\mu_{1}\mu_{2}=(ac)(bd)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p12.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b,d\}=\overline{\Sigma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b,d\}=\overline{\Sigma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S5.p12.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle of order 3 containing the two elements of
<math id="S5.p12.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Sigma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Sigma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The former case is impossible since <math id="S5.p12.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle(ab),\mu\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle(ab),\mu\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a primitive group. We conclude that the equivalence
class <math id="S5.p12.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(ab),(ab);1,\mu]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(ab),(ab);1,\mu]</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S5.p12.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(12),(12);1,(134)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>134</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(12),(12);1,(134)]</annotation></semantics></math>. The theorem is proved.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We can sharpen Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Theorem 3.14. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a> using the same proof and
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3 The case 
        
          
            ≥
            n
            
              -
              
                ⁢
                2
                d
              
              2 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>,  Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  4
                
              
              ,
              
                ≥
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5 The case 
        
          
            
              
              
              
            
            
              
                =
                n
                
                  -
                  
                    ⁢
                    2
                    d
                  
                  6
                
              
              ,
              
                =
                g
                1 ‣ Irreducibility of Hurwitz spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth, projective curve of genus <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2,\;n&gt;0,\;n\equiv 0(mod\;2)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime then
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)=\mathcal{H}^{0}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not prime
let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be its maximal divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\max(2,2d^{\prime}-4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\max(2,2d^{\prime}-4)</annotation></semantics></math> or if
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\max(2,2d^{\prime}-6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\max(2,2d^{\prime}-6)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the connected
components of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,n}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which correspond to simple coverings
with monodromy groups <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are in one-to-one correspondence with
the equivalence classes of unramified coverings <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\tilde{Y}\to Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tilde{Y}\to Y]</annotation></semantics></math>
of degrees <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}|d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}|d</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}\neq 1,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}\neq 1,d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
